# `model` is an optional per-role tier, propagated into the generated Claude
# Code wrapper's `model:` frontmatter by internal/generators/plugin_generation.go. Assigned
# by a fixed heuristic, not per-role discretion — keep new roles consistent
# with it:
#   opus:   design/architecture/governance/crypto-assurance roles making
#           high-blast-radius or hard-to-reverse judgment calls.
#   sonnet: default tier — accountable engineering build/debug, security
#           engineering, review, testing, operations, incident coordination,
#           planning.
#   haiku:  narrow, single-purpose, low-ambiguity roles — cataloging,
#           stewardship, triage/escalation routing, and bounded execution
#           specialists. An authoring role (code, test, or document) qualifies
#           as an execution specialist when its scope is bounded to a
#           pre-approved slice, it works under a named accountable role that
#           retains design and scope ownership, and it holds no approval,
#           deployment, or risk-acceptance authority.
#
#           Blast radius overrides that: an execution specialist is sonnet,
#           not haiku, when it is the *sole* author of artifacts that once
#           merged (a) execute with kernel or elevated privilege, (b) change
#           network reachability or security posture, (c) mutate persistent
#           infrastructure or data durability, (d) handle cryptographic
#           material, or (e) perform destructive or history-rewriting
#           operations. Execution routes select the specialist as sole
#           primary with the accountable role in `support`, so on those routes
#           no higher-tier agent co-authors — the tier of the specialist is
#           the tier of the work. UI/styling, tests and fixtures, build
#           configuration, and application-level code in memory-safe
#           languages stay haiku.
#
# `codex_model` is the OpenAI/Codex CLI equivalent, propagated into the
# generated `.toml` wrapper's `model` key. Codex does not share Claude's
# haiku/sonnet/opus naming, so this is a separate field, not a reused value —
# same tier mapping, OpenAI model identifiers:
#   opus -> gpt-5.6-sol    sonnet -> gpt-5.6-terra    haiku -> gpt-5.6-luna
# User-supplied mapping (not independently verified from inside this repo —
# see below); previously gpt-5 / gpt-5-codex / gpt-5-mini. Re-verify these
# identifiers against current Codex CLI docs before relying on them in
# automation; OpenAI model names change over time and this repo has no live
# check against Codex's accepted model list. Field-confirmed: a
# ChatGPT-authenticated (not API-key) Codex session rejected both of the
# previous gpt-5-codex and gpt-5 identifiers as "not supported" when passed
# as an explicit `--model`/spawn_agent override — two different tiers
# failing identically points more at that auth mode restricting any
# explicit model override than at those specific identifiers having been
# wrong, so switching to the identifiers above may not by itself resolve a
# ChatGPT-authenticated session's rejection; this repo cannot confirm that
# distinction without a live codex binary. See the "Codex CLI" section of
# .agents/skills/run-agent-orchestration/references/runner-adapters.md for
# the current dispatch-time workaround.
#
# `reasoning_effort` is a shared per-role value (not split per runner like
# model/codex_model above) propagated to both generated wrappers: Claude
# Code's `effort:` frontmatter field and Codex's `model_reasoning_effort`
# TOML field. Both runners accept the same low/medium/high/xhigh vocabulary
# (Claude Code additionally accepts `max`; Codex additionally accepts
# `minimal`, `ultra`, and a model-dependent `max` — none of those four are
# used here, keeping every value valid on both runners), so one field is
# sufficient. Verified 2026-07-28 against Claude Code's live docs
# (code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents) and Codex CLI's live docs
# (developers.openai.com/codex/agent-configuration/subagents,
# .../codex/config-file/config-reference) plus a real installed
# `@openai/codex` npm package's `codex exec --help` output — see
# internal/orchestration/dispatch_core.go's BuildChildArgv for how the MCP dispatch
# path passes this (Codex has no dedicated exec-time flag for it, only a
# generic `-c key=value` config override). Same tier mapping as model/
# codex_model above:
#   opus -> high    sonnet -> medium    haiku -> low
